# Task Manager Assignment Rules

This project is a **Senior Angular Developer assignment** with specific requirements.

## State Management

- **Use @ngrx/signals (SignalStore)** for state management
- Do NOT use traditional NgRx (Store/Actions/Reducers/Effects)
- Use Angular Signals for reactive state
- Use HTTPResource for API requests

## Change Detection

- **Use OnPush change detection strategy** on all components (requirement from assignment)
- Do NOT use zoneless mode (OnPush is explicitly required)
- Combine OnPush with Signals for optimal performance

## Component Architecture

- **Smart/Dumb (Presentational) component pattern**
  - Smart components: Handle state, business logic, use SignalStore
  - Dumb components: Pure presentation, inputs/outputs only, OnPush
- All components must be **Standalone Components**
- Use Angular Signals throughout

## Styling

- Use **Angular Material** for components
- Use **Tailwind CSS** for custom styling
- Implement responsive design (mobile, tablet, desktop)
- Include loading states and skeleton screens
- Add smooth animations and transitions

## Forms

- Use **Reactive Forms** only
- Include custom validators
- Proper error handling and display
- Form state management with Signals

## Performance

- OnPush change detection on all components
- Lazy loading for feature modules
- Proper RxJS usage (avoid memory leaks, use takeUntilDestroyed)
- TrackBy functions for lists
- Code splitting

## Testing

- Minimum **80% code coverage**
- Unit tests for components and services
- Use proper mocking strategies

## Code Quality

- Follow Angular Style Guide
- ESLint with Angular recommended rules
- Prettier for formatting
- JSDoc comments for complex logic
- Conventional commits for Git messages

## Key Requirements to Remember

- Modern Angular features (v20): Signals, Standalone, HTTPResource
- Smart/Presentational pattern
- Performance optimization is critical
- Testing coverage matters
- Clean, documented code
